At day 20, 1,006 people remain susceptible, while 3,389 are infectious, so there is still substantial remaining force of infection. Vaccinating 50% of the whole population means roughly half of the remaining susceptibles are protected, but many vaccinations go to already-infected or recovered people. With 95% efficacy, the effective remaining susceptible pool is cut to about 500–550 “unvaccinated-equivalent” people, making transmission subcritical after vaccination, but the large number of current infectious people should still infect many of the remaining unvaccinated susceptibles and a smaller fraction of vaccinated susceptibles.

My forecast is therefore centered around about 500 additional infections, with uncertainty from stochastic contacts, vaccine allocation among susceptibles, and infectious-duration timing.

