From day 20 to day 60, the epidemic is in its “tail” but still continuing: by day 20, 3994 of 5000 have ever been infected, leaving about 1006 still susceptible. With permanent immunity and no travel, the final size is at most 5000, so newly infected between day 20 and 60 is at most ~1006 and is likely a large fraction of that remaining pool. Given the remaining susceptible fraction (~20%) and that interventions start only on day 21 (with 50% coverage and strong efficacy), the second wave after day 21 should be substantially damped but not eliminated (vaccination doesn’t guarantee infection prevention; it reduces risk per exposure by 95%). I therefore expect a modest but nontrivial number of additional infections by day 60, perhaps on the order of a few hundred, with uncertainty depending on how much transmission occurs before vaccination fully matters and on stochastic contact/extinction timing.

Below are calibrated-looking quantiles consistent with “remaining susceptible ≈1006” and “strong but incomplete post–day-21 suppression,” yielding a distribution centered around a few hundred additional infections.

{"p10":120,"p25":240,"p50":340,"p75":460,"p90":610}

At day 20, about 2,684 people remain never-infected. A day-21 50%-coverage, 95%-efficacy campaign roughly splits those susceptibles into ~1,340 unvaccinated and ~1,340 vaccinated-but-mostly-protected. Because there are still ~1,944 infectious people, substantial additional spread is likely, especially among the unvaccinated, but post-vaccination effective transmission should be near or below replacement and the outbreak should largely burn out by day 60.

My forecast allows for stochastic variation in contact/transmission events and some uncertainty about infection-age structure/timing of the vaccination within day 21.

